motion rate and the lowest nickel usage in the league.","offense":"The Rams offense was about average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest pre-snap motion rate and the most receivers on the field in the league. Matthew Stafford ran the passing game, with Puka Nacua leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Rams had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, takeaways per game, only ranked 14th, and it was 27th in success rate allowed. Kobie Turner was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Braden Fiske, who had 8.5 sacks while playing only 59% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the lowest nickel usage in the league.","special":"The Rams were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 85% of field goals, which was a little above average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 83% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Matthew Stafford played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,762 yards. Targets were spread around, with Puka Nacua and Cooper Kupp leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Kyren Williams handled most of the work in the backfield with 76% of the carries. On defense, Braden Fiske led the team with 8.5 sacks and Kamren Kinchens picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Sean McVay called the offense as head coach and Chris Shula called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2022 and 2025 Lions. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Cardinals under Nick Rallis and the 2020 Packers.","trends":"The offense went from 9th in EPA per play in 2023 to 16th in 2024, a modest decline. The defense moved from 22nd in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 25th in 2024, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was play-action rate, which went from 18% to 31%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}